A||||14||BN Boomer||Fiero newbie seeks advice
Z||000000||BN Boomer||06-18-99||02:24 PM||||I recently bought an 87 Fiero with the 4 cylinder engine and automatic transmission. The car has 171,000 miles on it and is in pretty decent shape for it's age and mileage but the engine has a knocking rod, which is why I got it for $300. I figure for that price along with a little wreching this will make a fun little car for either my 16yo daughter to drive or me to use for commuting. <p>The rebuilding or replacing of the engine is where I'd like to pick your collective brains. I'd like to keep the costs down so I plan on sticking with the 4 cylinder motor. As far as rebuilding the current engine, it has a really bad knock but hasn't let go yet. After reading many unfavorable comments about the 2.5L, is rebuilding a worthwhile endeavor? This isn't going to be a vehicle used hard but I do expect and appreciate relibility. I see the Fiero Store has a 4 cyl rebuild kit for $489. Any comments about it or them? I'd like to think I can rebuild a 4 banger for less than a small block Chevy. How about mild perfomance upgrades for the 4 cyl? Any suggestions? If the engine proves too damaged to be rebuilt, will any "Tech IV" GM 2.5L FWD engine work in the Fiero?<p> Lastly, having never worked on a mid-engine vehicle, how difficult is the engine to remove? Dropping the whole engine/transmission assembly out the bottom doesn'y excite me too much. Does the 4 cyl come out the top easily? Any tips on facilitating this method?<p> 
Z||000001||Motorhead||06-18-99||03:11 PM||||If your not looking to build a high performance car, my advice is to stick with the 2.5.  Parts are readily available and cheap, and the engine is EASY to work on.<p>Gas mileage is GREAT!<p>As far as dropping the engine and transaxle, don't let the fact that it comes out the bottom be a concern.  Yes you can pull the engine out the top, but after dropping my engine and trans out the bottom last weekend,  I can't figure why I was so concerened.  Piece of cake, and what's really nice is the access you have to the entire drive line for inspection and repair.<p>I did fabricate a dolly to drop the engine/trans cradle onto, but that was no big deal either.<p>As far as performance mods on the 4 banger, you're limited by the engines' designe. In my opinion the mods should be limited to intake and exhaust improvements.<p>In the 4-cylinder form the little car is fun to drive, and an eye catcher, even after so many years!<p>Welcome to the club!
Z||000002||batboy||06-18-99||07:42 PM||||Motorhead is right on the money with his advice (in my humble opinion). The 2.5 liter is put down by a lot of people (mainly due to it's supposed lack of power), but many others lovingly refer to it as "the iron duke" because they are reliable if treated with kindness and regular oil changes. Like any motor, they're not perfect. Sometimes they will develop oil leaks, but they get great fuel mileage. My four cylinder Fiero consistantly gets 33 to 35 mpg on the highway. They never were designed to be a high performance engine, yet they will beat at least half of the cars out there away from the stoplight and are fun to drive. When you rebuild them, sometimes you'll find cracks in the head, if so then replace it. The machine shop bills will probably run at least $500 plus add the $500 for the rebuild kit. A cheaper and easier way to go is to buy a remanufactured long-block from a reputable company. Another option is to buy a good, low-mileage engine (they used those engines in a bunch of different GM cars over a long period of time). An even easier thing to do is to sell me that car for $500 and pocket a $200 profit (hehehe).
Z||000003||Stroker||06-18-99||09:29 PM||||   Batboy, you oughta be ashamed tryin' to wrench that guys car away so quickly!! Besides, I thought it was more appropriate to welcome him to the club first. THEN, buy it!!! Besides, I'll give him $550!<p>  BN Boomer, WELCOME TO THE FAMILY!!!!!<br>And besides anything said here, don't sell out! You'd be missing ALL the FUN!!!!!<br> Try looking for your rebuild kit through Performance Automotive Warehouse in Chatsworth, CA. They have most every rebuild kit imaginable, and CHEAP too!<br>  They are also on the web.<br>       <br>         Again, welcome and God bless!<p>  
Z||000004||batboy||06-18-99||09:58 PM||||Yes, welcome...ok, I now bid $600...Stroker is right about PAW, they sell good name brand parts at a good price. I've bought lots of engine goodies from them. You can find their ads in most automotive magazines.
Z||000005||Harry||06-19-99||06:15 AM||||You came to the right place to find an answer! Everybody here is very helpful and really enjoy there Fiero's<br>Welcome to the group, many happy miles<br>Ill buy it for 700 ( you guys are BAD)!
