Z||000008||Hock||06-21-99||12:26 PM||||Ok you guys are really obnoxus. The poor guy asled a simple question. So here is an anwser. For a cheap rebuild Northern Autoparts Wharehouse has cheap rebuild kits four the 2.5L. Speed Pro has some high lift flat tappet cams but Comp Cam should have the ones that you are looking for as low cost. There should be little work that needs to be done to install it piston clearence should be OK if you don't go with the 260H. If you look around you shold be able to find what you are looking for. good luck
Z||000009||theogre||06-21-99||01:17 PM||||Make sure any parts you get are correct for the 87. There were a number of significant changes to the motor in 86 & again in 87. The 86+ motors use a diferant cam, and in 87 the motor added DIS distributorless ignition and changed to a 700 series fuel injector body. The 700 series TBI is a completely diferant design.<p>You can verify the kit by checking against other GM cars like Pontiac 6000, Chevy Cavalier, and Old Cutlas Calais for 1987-89<p>You also have to be carefull when looking up some parts. There are a number of parts books that list the 86 parts as fitting the 87, and some things don't. PCV system parts are a good example. Practically every book I've looked in for 87 PCV parts is wrong.<p>Finally, 87-88 fiero had a number of other changes that only applied to one or both of those years. The ones that affect you most are body trim, and the headlight system. Starting with 87 GM went to a current sensing control box for the headligh motors, and as a result the motors last longer. The motors themselves also had improvments.<p>As for performance improvements, there are limits to what you can do to an 87. There are no alternates for the TBI, or addon/upgrade ignition parts that I've found yet. One basic thing you can do durring the rebuild is have the head ported and pollished. That is cheap and imporves flow considerably.
Z||000010||BN Boomer||07-07-99||01:21 PM||||Thanks for all the advice guys. Sorry, but my diamond in the rough that was rescued from a farmers field is not for sale. Hopefully I'll get going on this project at the end of this month. I've got catalogs coming from The Fiero Store and Northern Auto Parts. So far I like the looks of the rebuild kit from The Fiero Store as it appears they use "top shelf" quality parts. I'm going to do a little more research into what's available from NAP. Any additonal suggestions for other automobile parts sources?
